+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+Review date: 4 November 2026  
+Scope: the accounts-service API, its persistence, scheduled payment dispatch, and the scheme, fraud, ledger and notification integrations.
+
+This is a qualitative FMEA based on the current source and README, not on production incident frequencies. The RPN is `severity × occurrence × detection`; higher detection scores mean that the failure is harder to detect. Scores are 1 (low) to 10 (high). Severity considers customer, financial, regulatory and operational impact; occurrence considers the plausibility in normal operation; detection considers how likely the existing service controls are to identify the problem promptly.
+
+## Failure-mode analysis
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems and teams | Current detection / controls | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| F-01 | A payment is sent without fraud screening | `PaymentService` never calls `FraudScreeningClient`, despite FRD-7; queued dispatch also goes directly to the scheme | Fraudulent or mule payments can leave the account; customers may suffer loss and lose confidence | Fraud team has no screening event or review item; compliance breach and investigation workload; scheme and ledger process an unauthorised payment | Fraud client and test fake exist, but there is no runtime control or metric proving every payment was screened | 10 | 6 | 10 | 600 | Make screening a mandatory state transition before both immediate and queued dispatch. Fail closed on `REFER`, timeout and error; persist the decision and audit event; alert on any payment without one. |
+| F-02 | `REFER` or fraud-service failure is treated incorrectly | Missing screening orchestration means `REFER` cannot become `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`; unavailable/error cannot be converted to a hold | A referred payment may be sent, or a customer may receive no clear review status | Fraud team cannot meet the four-hour review SLA; policy, audit and regulatory evidence is incomplete | No hold queue, review workflow, fraud decision persistence or alert exists | 10 | 5 | 10 | 500 | Implement `ACCEPT`, `REFER` and `REJECT` handling explicitly. Store screening result, use `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` with `PAYMENT_UNDER_REVIEW`, and never send on timeout/error. |
+| F-03 | Duplicate payment is created and sent | POST has no idempotency key or uniqueness constraint; client retry creates a new payment id and therefore a new `P` scheme reference | Customer can be debited twice, with duplicate payment to the payee | Scheme settlement and ledger reconciliation break; operations and complaints team must investigate and recover funds | Scheme protects only repeats of the same reference; no request idempotency or duplicate-payment alert exists | 10 | 6 | 9 | 540 | Require a client idempotency key, persist it per tenant/account, enforce uniqueness, and return the original payment on retry. Add duplicate/payee/amount heuristics and reconciliation alerts. |
+| F-04 | A scheme timeout leaves an unknown payment outcome | `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught; the transaction rolls back locally even though the scheme may have accepted the unique `P{id}` instruction | Customer sees an error and may retry; the payee may receive money while the customer sees no local payment | Scheme settlement can be unmatched; ledger may not be posted; complaints and finance reconciliation cannot reliably resolve the payment | No status-query use, timeout state, durable outbox or exception report for scheme timeouts | 10 | 5 | 10 | 500 | Catch timeout, commit a durable `SUBMITTED`/`UNKNOWN` state, query the scheme by the same reference, and reconcile before any retry. Never generate a new scheme reference. |
+| F-05 | A scheme accepts an instruction but the local transaction is rolled back or a later dispatch repeats it | External scheme side effect occurs inside the database transaction; no outbox/state machine; retries are not coordinated | Payment may be made but disappear from the customer's history, or appear late; a later manual retry can create confusion | Settlement-to-ledger and payment-history breaks; operations cannot distinguish “not sent” from “sent” | Scheme reference is stable for a given payment, but there is no durable timeout/recovery workflow or status query | 9 | 4 | 10 | 360 | Use an outbox or committed state machine for external calls, with a reconciler and scheme status query. Treat unknown as a first-class state and expose it to operations. |
+| F-06 | Ledger posting times out after it may have booked | Ledger is called with a 5-second timeout; timeout may mean the debit was applied | Customer may see `POSTING_FAILED`, an incorrect local balance/history, or later be debited twice during remediation | Finance sees a scheme-sent/ledger-uncertain break; operations must manually repost; a new reference can double-debit | Timeout is marked `POSTING_FAILED` and logged, but there is no automatic status query or enforced same-reference retry | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | Persist `ledgerReference` before posting, provide an idempotent retry/reconciliation workflow using that exact reference, and alert on aged `POSTING_FAILED` records. |
+| F-07 | Ledger posting fails after the scheme has sent the payment | Scheme submission precedes ledger posting; ledger timeout/error is only partly handled | Payment leaves the account but the customer-facing record is failed or incomplete; balance and money movement can diverge | Finance reconciliation breaks; exceptions report grows; operations must resolve a real external payment and a missing/uncertain ledger debit | `POSTING_FAILED` status and warning log exist; no durable outbox, retry worker or age/SLA alert is visible | 10 | 5 | 7 | 350 | Make scheme/ledger reconciliation a supported workflow. Keep the original ledger reference, retry safely, and notify with a distinct failed/under-investigation template only after the outcome is known. |
+| F-08 | Concurrent payments overspend or exceed the daily limit | Balance and `paidToday` are read before save; no row lock, optimistic version, atomic reservation or unique request key | Two simultaneous requests can both pass checks and spend more than the available balance or limit | Ledger receives excess debits; account copy and finance reconciliation diverge; complaints and manual recovery follow | Single-request validation and an in-memory/H2 test setup do not protect production concurrency | 10 | 5 | 9 | 450 | Add transactional account locking/versioning and an atomic funds/limit reservation. Include concurrent-payment integration tests and reject/release reservations on every terminal state. |
+| F-09 | The service approves payment using a stale copied balance | `accounts.balance` is only a copy and is overwritten by the ledger at 02:00; payment checks do not consult the ledger | Payment can be refused despite funds, or accepted despite an overdrawn ledger balance | Ledger rejects or books an unexpected debit; finance and support investigate balance discrepancies | Nightly ledger overwrite is described, but no freshness timestamp, drift monitor, or pre-payment ledger check exists | 9 | 6 | 8 | 432 | Add balance freshness/version metadata and reconciliation. For exact decisions (especially closure and high-risk payments), check the ledger; define behavior when the copy is stale/unavailable. |
+| F-10 | A queued payment executes on a bank holiday or at the wrong working date | `WorkingDays` handles Monday-Friday only; bank holidays are explicitly not modelled | Payment is delayed or rejected with `DT01`, contrary to the promised queueing behavior | Scheme rejects the instruction; operations must reschedule and reconcile; customer support handles avoidable failures | Weekend check and cutoff test exist; no bank-holiday calendar or `DT01` monitoring | 8 | 5 | 8 | 320 | Use a versioned UK bank-holiday calendar (or scheme working-day API), calculate the next valid execution date, and alert on rejected queued payments. |
+| F-11 | A queued payment is missed, delayed, or only partly drained | 06:00 cron is the only dispatch trigger; no lease, retry, catch-up control, or per-payment error isolation; one uncaught failure can abort the job | Payment remains queued past its date and the customer is not paid when promised | Operations exception report and scheme settlement are incomplete; downstream payees and support are affected | Repository query can find overdue queued records, but no monitoring/metric/SLA alert or durable job run record is implemented | 8 | 5 | 8 | 320 | Make dispatch durable and repeatable: claim/lease rows, isolate failures, retry with backoff, run catch-up for overdue items, and alert on queued age and failed job runs. |
+| F-12 | A payment is charged locally even though the scheme rejects it | Account is debited only after a successful scheme response in the current path, but future changes/retries or partial transaction handling could violate this ordering; rejection itself is only persisted in the enclosing transaction | Customer's available balance can be wrong and they may be unable to make another payment | Ledger, account copy and scheme status disagree; support and finance need a correction | `SchemeRejectedException` sets `REJECTED` and returns before debit; no invariant test/monitor checks rejected payments never have ledger postings or debits | 9 | 3 | 7 | 189 | Enforce a state transition invariant and reconciliation rule: rejected/held/cancelled payments must have no debit or external posting. Add property/integration tests and compensating workflow. |
+| F-13 | Customer is told the wrong payment outcome or not told at all | Notification is sent only after dispatch; failures are caught and only logged; `POSTING_FAILED` uses the same `PAYMENT_SENT` notification path | Customer may believe a payment completed when ledger posting failed, or miss a queued/held/rejected status | Support receives avoidable contacts; notification service has no reliable delivery evidence; complaints history is incomplete | Best-effort catch prevents notification failure from undoing a payment; warning logs are the only visible control | 7 | 6 | 8 | 336 | Use status-specific templates (`QUEUED`, `UNDER_REVIEW`, `FAILED`, `SENT`), record notification outcome, add delivery metrics and a controlled retry/dead-letter process without coupling it to payment commit. |
+| F-14 | Payment or account data is disclosed across tenants or to an unauthorised role | Any regression in resource-level checks, token claim mapping, or a repository query without tenant filtering can expose records; payment view contains payee sort code/account number | Personal and financial data breach; customers can see another customer's payment details | Regulatory breach, incident response, complaints and loss of trust; support/admin access boundaries fail | JWT validation, tenant checks, 404-for-hidden-resource convention, and `AccessPolicy` cover current paths; tests cover core access cases but no systematic query-level or contract test is evident | 10 | 3 | 7 | 210 | Centralise tenant predicates, add negative tests for every endpoint and role/tenant combination, run automated data-leakage tests, and audit/redact logs and API payloads against data-minimisation requirements. |
+| F-15 | Sensitive internal notes, risk data, or case comments are exposed | Staff/customer projections or comment filtering can be bypassed as new endpoints are added; data model contains internal flags and risk scores | Customer privacy and safety can be harmed; fraud indicators may be revealed | Data-protection incident, fraud-team control weakness, complaints and regulatory work | Existing README and selected controller projections state staff-only rules; no common response filter or endpoint-wide authorization test suite is evident | 9 | 3 | 8 | 216 | Enforce role/resource policy in a shared service, use separate customer/staff DTOs, deny-by-default fields, and add tests proving `internal=true` and risk scores never cross the customer boundary. |
+| F-16 | Account is closed with money still in it | Closure workflow has request/list only; no shown approval execution or ledger zero-balance check; copied balance is not authoritative | Customer funds can be stranded and payments/access may stop unexpectedly | Ledger retains funds while service says closed; operations and finance must remediate manually; regulatory/complaints risk | Two-person request/listing exists, but no approval endpoint, ledger check, or closure reconciliation is present | 10 | 3 | 10 | 300 | Implement approval as a guarded state transition: re-authorize approver, query authoritative ledger balance, require zero and no unsettled payments, then close atomically; audit every decision. |
+| F-17 | Statements or transaction history are incomplete or wrong | No statement-generation/API workflow is present; payment dispatch debits the account copy but does not create a `Transaction`; statements are static fixtures | Customer cannot prove or understand account activity; monthly statement may omit payments | Regulatory return can be wrong; finance, complaints and support lack a complete audit trail | Read-only transaction and statement entities exist, but no completeness/reconciliation job or statement cut process is implemented | 9 | 5 | 9 | 405 | Build ledger-driven statement generation and payment transaction recording, make period creation idempotent, reconcile statement totals to ledger, and alert before the fifth working day deadline. |
+| F-18 | Time and date rules produce inconsistent records | Some domain constructors use `Instant.now()` directly (`ClosureRequest`, `SupportCase`) rather than the injected UK `Clock`; cutoff/queue logic depends on UK time and bank-holiday data | Requests, executions and support timelines can be timestamped incorrectly around DST or in tests; payments may queue/send on the wrong date | SLA, audit, statement and regulatory evidence can be inconsistent; operations cannot reliably order events | Payment service and job inject `Clock`; configuration fixes zone for the scheduler, but time use is not consistent across domains | 7 | 4 | 7 | 196 | Inject the clock into all domain services/factories, use one UK time policy, test DST/cutoff/weekend/holiday boundaries, and persist event timestamps consistently. |
+| F-19 | Authentication or authorization configuration fails open or is misconfigured | Public-key, issuer/claim validation, role/scope mapping or tenant claims are misconfigured; deployment configuration differs from tested settings | Unauthorised reads/changes or complete customer outage | Cross-tenant breach, payment fraud, support outage and incident response | Bearer JWT validation, stateless security, coarse endpoint rules, method security and 401/403 conventions exist; no issuer/audience configuration or deployment drift control is shown | 10 | 2 | 8 | 160 | Validate issuer, audience, expiry and required claims; fail closed on missing/invalid tenant or roles; manage keys/configuration centrally, test authorization in deployment, and alert on auth failures and claim anomalies. |
+| F-20 | Database or service outage loses availability or a committed record | H2/in-memory configuration is suitable for tests but not durable production operation; database, connection pool, deployment or migration failure can make reads/writes unavailable | Customers cannot view balances, make payments, open cases or obtain statements | Mobile/online channels, support, fraud operations, finance and regulatory reporting lose service; payment requests may be retried | `/actuator/health` is public and basic application health exists; no HA, backup/restore, migration, SLO or recovery evidence is in the repository | 9 | 3 | 8 | 216 | Use a durable HA database, backups and restore tests, safe migrations, readiness/liveness probes, graceful degradation, idempotent retries and service-level alerts aligned to 07:00–23:00/99.9% targets. |
+| F-21 | A payment is retried with a new ledger reference after an uncertain post | Dispatcher generates a fresh UUID each dispatch; operational guidance says retries must reuse the original reference, but no code enforces it | Customer can be debited twice while seeing one payment or a failure | Ledger and reconciliation double-post; finance and operations have to reverse or recover funds | `ledgerReference` is stored after scheme submission, but it is overwritten with a new UUID on every dispatch and there is no retry API guard | 10 | 4 | 9 | 360 | Generate and persist the ledger reference once before the first post; make retries refuse to proceed if the reference is absent/changed, and expose a controlled same-reference retry operation. |
+
+## Highest-priority release actions
+
+The largest current risks are F-01/F-02 (unscreened payments), F-03 (duplicate POSTs), F-04/F-05 (unknown scheme outcomes), F-08 (concurrent spending), F-09 (stale balance), F-16 (unsafe closure), and F-17 (incomplete financial records). These should be treated as release-blocking until the corresponding controls and tests exist.
+
+At minimum, release evidence should include:
+
+- an end-to-end payment state machine covering fraud, scheme timeout/status query, ledger timeout/idempotent retry, queueing, rejection and notification failure;
+- concurrency and retry tests proving one customer request produces at most one scheme instruction and one ledger debit;
+- daily scheme/ledger/account reconciliation with aged-exception alerts;
+- tenant/role negative tests and a data-minimisation review of payment and notification payloads;
+- authoritative-ledger checks for closure and a statement/transaction completeness control; and
+- operational dashboards for held payments, overdue queued payments, `POSTING_FAILED`, unknown scheme outcomes, notification failures and job runs.
